<.shell title="Open in maps" current={:open_in_maps}>
  <h1>Open in maps</h1>
  <p class="lead">A place in Google Maps or Apple Maps, or copied.</p>

  <div class="demo max-w-sm pb-16">
    <dl>
      <dt>Address</dt>
      <dd>Murrin Provincial Park, Squamish</dd>
      <dt>Latitude and longitude</dt>
      <dd>
        49.64310,-123.20890
        <.open_in_maps
          id="open-in-maps-demo"
          coordinate="49.64310,-123.20890"
          address="Murrin Provincial Park, Squamish"
          label="Rope rescue practice"
        />
      </dd>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <h2>When to use</h2>
  <p>
    Under an activity's latitude and longitude, or its address when it has none, and under a member's address.
  </p>

  <h2>When not to use</h2>
  <p>
    In a list row that is already a link, such as a <a href={page_path(:row_link)}>row link</a>: a link cannot hold a button.
  </p>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;.open_in_maps id="…" coordinate=&#123;…&#125; address=&#123;…&#125; label=&#123;…&#125; /&gt;</code>. With a latitude and longitude it pins that spot, and Apple Maps labels the pin with <code>label</code>. Google Maps cannot. Without one it searches for the address. Never label a member's address with their name.
  </p>
  <p>
    It opens Google Maps until someone selects Apple Maps in the menu. The browser remembers that choice for every map button on the device. The menu also copies the latitude and longitude or the address, for a GPS app.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is a split button, <code>&lt;span class="btn-split"&gt;</code>
    around two <code>&lt;.button&gt;</code>s, with a <code>.menu.menu-start</code>
    under it.
  </p>
</.shell>
